jabraw (0.4.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop reading every single byte as a switch. Settings that Jabra's device
    model files describe as a choice keep their number, rather than being
    labelled off or on — soundMode 0 is bass, not off. See
    doc/settings-sources.md for where those files are and what they leave open.
  * Give the desktop entries an English default with a German translation.
  * Clean up the icon cache when the package is removed.
  * Build both packaging paths in CI and compare what they ship, run the
    autopkgtest against the installed package, and check formatting, lints and
    tests on every push.

 -- Michael Dold <michidold@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 17:07:34 +0200

jabraw (0.4.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Keep an RFCOMM session when the headset does not know a subcommand; only a
    broken socket ends it now. A device that reports its battery but not its
    name reconnected in a loop and never got as far as the battery.
  * Ship the manual page, copyright and changelog in the .deb, register the
    autostart entry as a conffile, and strip the binary.
  * Format the tree with rustfmt, and check formatting, lints and tests on
    every push.

 -- Michael Dold <michidold@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 12:21:02 +0200

jabraw (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Put a deadline on every D-Bus call; zbus waits for a reply without one.
  * Run the whole RFCOMM session beside the main loop, and ask a headset for
    its identity once per session whether it answers or not.
  * Fail the package build when a library dependency cannot be determined.
  * Build the release without write access to the repository and audit the
    dependencies before publishing.
  * Add unit tests for the paths that parse what the device sends.

 -- Michael Dold <michidold@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 11:20:00 +0200

jabraw (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the RFCOMM profile registration, which left the daemon unable to
    reconnect over Bluetooth and stalled its loop on every attempt.
  * Run the dialogs beside the main loop, cap the audio helpers, bound the
    internal queues and read the headset only when something can change.
  * Require an authenticated Bluetooth link, match the incoming socket to the
    device it was asked for, and escape what the device calls itself.

 -- Michael Dold <michidold@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 10:46:36 +0200

